now it was an extraordinary Verdict by a  coroner that promises to change social  media and do a huge amount to protect  children from online harm the coroner  said 14 year old Molly Russell died from  an act of self-harm while suffering from  depression and the negative effects of  online content in other words social  media was partly to blame for Molly's  death it was a crucial victory for Ian  Russell her father who had fought a long  battle to hear those words after the  inquest he said this  we should not be sitting here this  should not happen because it does not  need to happen  um  but we told this story in the hope that  change  would come about I hope  that the world  will be safer the digital world  particularly will be a safer place to  inhabit  and the final  thing I want to say  is thank you Molly  for being my daughter  a hugely emotional moment but are things  really going to change or are millions  of children still at grave risk well Ian  Russell is here with me now thank you  for coming in and you fought an  extraordinary battle  um to get this inquest verdict a very  significant verdict but six weeks on do  you think things really will change  that's such a such an important question  mark  um I think they have to change they have  to change for the sake of our young  people  um there's still an awful lot of harmful  content available online very easily  available and in fact  um there was a survey published by  Samaritans only last week uh survey  commissioned by um the Samaritans and  Saint by Swansea University  um that said over three quarters of  young people had come across self-harm  content by the time they were 14 and of  of that number of people uh 83 percent  um didn't go searching for that content  it was pushed to them by the by the  platform's algorithms so they they were  confronted with content harmful content  that they didn't even want to see so so  for viewers who don't know  um what kind of content was Molly  exposed to  it's very hard because as a broadcaster  you're not allowed to show a lot of the  content Molly uh was exposed to  newspapers can't print it you can't  broadcast it and yet it's readily  available on platforms that officially  have a lower age limit of 13.  but this is material almost encouraging  yeah there's material that normalizes  self-harm there's material  um that shows graphic self-harm wounds  and that's very disturbing to see  um quite often uh quite gory quite  bloody and not pleasant to see but then  there's material that  um encourages sort of depressive  thinking uh a sense of worthlessness  which I think is actually the more  dangerous of the two sorts of material  they're both horrible  but that sense that Morley had as we  know because she left us some notes that  she felt hopeless and worthless and she  felt a burden to other people when in  fact it was completely the opposite is  the material that encouraged that sense  in her that said simply uh things a  little cartoon drawing I say this got  often the one that sticks in my mind is  a cartoon drawing of a sad looking uh  girl that just said who would love a  suicidal girl  and it wasn't just that one post which  on its own might not be too harmful or  dangerous but Molly saw hundreds if not  thousands of those posts in the last  weeks and months of her life and this  stuff is still out there  um the chief executive of nspcc said the  other day more than a month after the  coroner concluded social media  contributed to the death of Molly the  sites our children use  are still Awash with dangerous self-harm  and suicide content because there are no  consequences for industry uh in action  so  um what are the chances and I know this  Online safety bill is going through the  process but it is being delayed and it  looks as if it might be watered down how  worried are you about that  that is a massive worry and it's not  just a worry for me sadly I I've met too  many other parents in very similar  circumstances the the official stats in  the UK show that there are on average  four school-aged children that die by  suicide every week that's four mollies  four families like wine four sets of  friends who've lost someone so the age  of self-regulation has failed and the  only thing that will change that is  government regulation to to to make the  tech platforms put their house in order  the third reading of this bill which has  been well considered there was public  consultation there was a joint scrutiny  committee that looked at the bill and  made sure that it was as good as  possible before it went to the House of  Commons the third reading of that bill  in the Commons should have happened in  July but because of the political  turmoil in the government at the time  change of leadership it was delayed  should have come back in early November  it's been delayed again and we are  waiting for a date when that will happen  and the problem is that that bill has to  get through the commons and then the  House of Lords have to have enough time  to scrutinize it because it should be  scrutinized in both change Chambers  that's how this thing works in our  country and so that normal process of of  uh uh scrutiny by the House of Lords is  it it needs to have enough time to do  its job right and I mean it's going very  slowly and it is being watered down  particularly in terms of this harmful  but legal content and they're now  talking about allowing it for adults but  not for children now that then relies on  this whole idea of a proper age  verification so I mean how concerned are  you that the tech companies will in a  sense be led off the hook  um  I am concerned that there's a  possibility that tech companies will be  led off the hook  and that horrifies me because I know  what I've lived through and I don't want  anyone else to have to live through what  I've lived through and as the coroner  found social media played a part in  Molly's death uh so this has to stop  it's really important that protections  for Children online are at the highest  possible levels we protect ourselves in  our offline world we have speed limits  on motorways and we have seat belts in  cars and airbags  um we acknowledge the dangers and we  protect ourselves we need to start doing  that online as well so if this doesn't  get through in this parliamentary term  would you feel personally let down would  you say would you think the government  had let you down I would think the  government has let not just me down  but every parent in this country down if  the Online safety bill in a meaningful  way isn't given enough time to be  debated properly and to be on the  statute books so that oftcom the  regulator who are waiting in the wings  ready to do this they're keen and eager  they've already got staff working on how  they're going to regulate this they're  ready to go this just needs to get a  move on get through Parliament so that  children and young people in particular  can be protected online and the stats  are mind-numbing more than two-thirds  British children age five to Fifteen  using social media you you know hinted  at this one third of seeing upsetting  content of some sort I mean these are  huge numbers and just to be clear that  Molly's behavior in the real world Ian  gave you new no indication that she was  inhabiting this Bleak online world that  you knew nothing about there was no  indication that there was any uh mental  health problems with Mom beforehand she  was a she contributed  um in a normal way to our family life  she was really happy she she may have  spent a little bit more time in her room  as her older sisters had done before her  she was a teenager she may she did come  to me and say I I'd like to give up  horse riding which with withdrawing from  Hobbies is a sign to look out for but  she didn't just say I'm bored with it  she said I want to give up horse riding  because I plateaued and I think by  having a rest and going back to it it  would be better for me there was always  a forward-looking thing the weekend  before she died she was looking forward  to her birthday which uh was on the 21st  of November she would have been 20 this  year she was looking forward to her 15th  birthday she'd written a birthday list  she bought  um presents for her sisters who also had  November birthdays she was so  forward-looking and so positive it was a  bolt from the blue it was a horror story  for us so I mean so there are so many  questions that you have  that may never be answered  I think that's always the case after a  suicide and one of the other things I  think the the Online safety bill has to  talk about is the process by which the  authorities not necessarily the family  but the authorities can gain data we  struggle during the inquest process  which is one of the reasons it took so  long we had a very supportive coroner  who who did everything within his power  to get data from the platform so that we  could look into Molly's viewing habits  it took ages for them to give that data  to his inquest that's easier now or  would it be easier under an online  safety bill it needs to be and there  needs to be measures in the bill that  there's almost a sort of automatic  process where  um a coroner or a law of official law  enforcement request when they are  received the tech companies  automatically preserve and deliver the  data to the authorities in the same way  that a police officer can come to your  house with a search warrant and search  your premises they need the search  warrant but they can do that the same  thing again has to happen in the online  world now finally uh Ian there will be  other mollies out there there will be  other  um youngsters in a similar situation do  you have any advice what would your  advice be to parents who  maybe listening to this or know what  happened and are deeply worry what would  your advice be to those parents it's  it's really hard because you have 